  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 AM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nDespite mostly clear skies early this morning, temps are holding\nin the mid 50s to lower 60s thanks to strong low level winds\njust above the surface. Temps will likely remain nearly \nstationary until daybreak and begin a steady climb into the \nlower 80s for highs today for most locations. Southerly winds \nwill gust into the 20-25 mph range at times today and favor a \nsoutheasterly direction this afternoon, which will keep temps \ncooler near the IL Lake Michigan shore.\n\nThe remnants of Isaias are expected to pass generally east of\nthe local area with perhaps just a few showers across northwest\nIN tonight into Sunday morning and its possible much of\nnorthwest IN will remain dry. Low temps tonight look to drop\ninto the 50s for most areas. Temps will be a few degrees cooler\non Sunday for the eastern parts of the cwa and easterly winds\nmay keep areas near Lake Michigan a few degrees cooler, but\ntemps well inland across the western parts of the area may still\nreach the upper 70s/near 80 Sunday afternoon.\n\nAn upper ridge will build across the area Sunday night into\nMonday bringing a very warm day to the region as high temps are\nexpected to reach the lower to mid 80s across the entire area. \nSouthwest winds may also gust into the 20-30 mph range. A \npossible limiting factor for both the temps and the winds will \nbe cloud cover. But with low temps only dropping into the upper \n50s/lower 60s by Monday morning, highs in the lower/mid 80s is \ncertainly possible and in line with guidance. If these temps \nwere to materialize, they will be close to record highs for \nOctober 12.\n\nA cold front is expected to move across the area starting early\nTuesday morning and then likely pushing south of the area by\nearly/mid afternoon. Its speed/timing will have a large impact \non temps which may still reach the upper 70s/lower 80s across \nthe southern cwa. Precipitation chances look fairly low during \nthis time period, but a few showers and possibly a few \nthunderstorms are possible. Still appears a storm system will \ndevelop over the Plains midweek, which will lift a warm front \nback across the area with one or two cold fronts moving across \nthe area late next week. Still quite a bit of uncertainty for \nhow this will all unfold, but the ensembles still show good \nconsistency for a push of much colder air next weekend. cms",
